==German==

===Etymology===
Burenwurst gained popularity at the time of the second [[w:Boer war|Boer war]] between 1899 and 1902. That's why the sausage received its name in connection to the Boers (Buren). Austrians at that time were so impressed about the staying power of the Boers during the war against the mighty British Empire in South Africa, that they named a sausage after the Boers i.e. Boer-wurst which in Austrian translates to and began to be known as "Burenwurst" as a sign of solidaraty..

In Vienna the Burenwurst can be also called as Burenhäutl (Boer Skin). It's the most important sausage in Viennese snack bars.   The original Burenwurst is never is never grilled or baked, only cooked. In Lindz; capital of Upper Austria, Burenwurst is served with sweet mustard at local street stalls and snack bars. 

This sausage differes from its South African counterpart the "Boerewors" (farmer's sausage - Afrikans) in that it has a finer texture and consistancy whereas the South African Boerewors is far courser in texture of meat and fat.  Both sausages are loved by many and consumed with great gusto.

Today the Burenwurst is still the most popular snack wurst sold in the Vienna street stalls, snack bars and cafe's.

